---
import { calculateSourceReversal, type SourceReversal } from "../lib/alignment-engine";
import type { Mode } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { scenario: SourceReversal; mode: Mode }
const { scenario, mode } = Astro.props;
const result = calculateSourceReversal(scenario);
const sourceCount = scenario.sourceTokens.length;
---
<section class="mass-ledger alignment-ledger" aria-labelledby="source-reversal-heading">
  <header class="ledger-header"><div><p class="eyebrow">Synthetic example · source order only</p><h2 id="source-reversal-heading">Which pairs move closer?</h2></div><p class="ledger-folio">Source reversal<br />§3.3 · p. 4</p></header>
  <div class="ledger-prompt"><p><strong>{mode === "beginner" ? "Predict first." : "Derive first."}</strong> {mode === "beginner" ? "A is paired with W; D is paired with Z. Does reversing the source shorten both gaps?" : "Keep original source identities. Derive each target-minus-source slot gap and its mean, then test whether the mean must stay equal for other pair sets."}</p></div>
  <div class="trace-intro"><p>Read each strip left to right, then continue into the target. Reverse source words only; its EOS stays at the boundary. The target order and declared pairs stay fixed.</p><p class="provenance-note mt-3">These authored symbols and pairs illustrate temporal distance. They are not measured alignments, hidden states, gradients or translation outputs. A gap counts slot transitions, including the source EOS; target EOS is displayed but has no paired source word.</p></div>
  {[["Forward source", result.forwardOrder], ["Reversed source", result.reversedOrder], ["Unchanged target", result.targetOrder]].map(([label, tokens]) => <div class="trace-history"><h3>{label}</h3><ol class="reversal-slots" aria-label={label as string}>{(tokens as string[]).map((token, index) => <li><strong>{token}</strong><span>slot {label === "Unchanged target" ? sourceCount + 1 + index : index}</span></li>)}</ol></div>)}
  <div class="trace-history">
    <table class="trace-table reversal-gaps"><caption>Same declared pairs · gaps in slot transitions</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Pair</th><th scope="col">Forward</th><th scope="col">Reversed</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      {scenario.pairs.map((pair, index) => <tr><th scope="row">{scenario.sourceTokens[pair.sourceIndex]} → {scenario.targetTokens[pair.targetIndex]}</th><td>{result.forwardGaps[index]}</td><td>{result.reversedGaps[index]}</td></tr>)}
      <tr class="trace-result"><th scope="row">Mean</th><td>{result.forwardMean}</td><td>{result.reversedMean}</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p class="mt-3">A → W shortens from 5 to 2 transitions. D → Z lengthens from 5 to 8. The mean stays 5 for these four pairs: a shorter early path does not mean every path gets shorter.</p>
    <details class="trace-arithmetic"><summary>Inspect slot arithmetic and the mean boundary</summary><p>With zero-based original source index i, target index j and S = {sourceCount} source words, target slot is S + 1 + j. Forward source slot is i; reversed source slot is S − 1 − i.</p><p class="mt-3">A → W: forward 5 − 0 = 5; reversed 5 − 3 = 2. D → Z: forward 8 − 3 = 5; reversed 8 − 0 = 8. Forward mean = (5 + 5 + 5 + 5) / 4 = 5; reversed mean = (2 + 4 + 6 + 8) / 4 = 5.</p><p class="mt-3">Equal source-position coverage gives the equal mean here. Retain only A → W and the means become 5 versus 2. The paper’s unchanged-average explanation is not a theorem for arbitrary alignments or sentence lengths.</p></details>
  </div>
  <div class="trace-comparison"><details class="prediction-reveal"><summary>Check early, late and average distance</summary><p>The first pair gets closer and the last gets farther apart; their changes balance in this example. A short gap is a property of this declared sequence layout. It does not measure a gradient’s magnitude or prove the mechanism behind an experimental gain.</p></details></div>
</section>
